# Documentation Standards Rule

Always add comprehensive documentation for new code and modules.

## File-Level Documentation
- Every new file must start with a header comment explaining the module.
- Place it before imports; use clear language on purpose and responsibilities.
- Include when/how the module should be used.

## Documentation Format
- Prefer text blocks over inline comments; use multi-line blocks for complex parts.
- Keep inline comments minimal.
- Use JSDoc for TS/JS; docstrings for Python.

## Required Documentation Elements
- Module purpose and rationale
- Key responsibilities (main functions/classes)
- Usage context (when/how to use)
- Dependencies/requirements
- Examples for complex modules

## Code Documentation Standards
- Document all public functions/classes/methods.
- Explain complex algorithms or business logic.
- Document non-obvious implementation details.
- Include parameter and return descriptions.
- Add TODOs for known limitations or future improvements.

## File Creation Guidelines
- **NEVER create .md files (README, documentation, guides, etc.) unless explicitly instructed by the user.**
- **NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files. Only create documentation files if explicitly requested by the User.**
- Only create code files when explicitly requested.
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones.

## Enforcement
- All new files must include proper documentation.
- Update documentation when files are modified or functionality changes.
- Review documentation quality during code reviews.